course: dictionary
lesson: 2161
title: Corner: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A corner is where two sides or edges meet. A square has 4 corners, and a triangle has 3. In maths, corners are also called vertices. When a word has more than one meaning, the sentence around it tells you which meaning to choose.
explain: «She rings the school, and the next week a teacher stands at that corner at half past eight.» Here corner is a place, the spot where two streets meet. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Corner can also be a verb. «He rode off round the corner and didn't come back.» Round the corner is a place, but the word cornered is different. To corner someone means to trap them so they cannot get away. If the cat cornered the mouse, the mouse had nowhere left to run.
fact: A corner is the point where two sides or edges meet.
fact: A square has 4 corners, and a triangle has 3.
fact: In maths, corners are also called vertices.
fact: «Corner» can mean a place where two streets meet, as in «the store on the corner».
fact: To corner someone means to trap them so they cannot get away.
exercise: How many corners does a square have? | 3 | 2 | *4 | 8
exercise: «She rings the school, and the next week a teacher stands at that corner at half past eight.» What is «that corner» most likely? | A square box | *A place where two streets meet | A kick in football | A bend in a river
exercise: The cat cornered the mouse. What does cornered mean? | Gave the mouse a treat | *Trapped it so it could not get away | Lost it in the grass | Chased it up a tree slowly
test: In «He rode off round the corner and didn't come back», what does corner most likely mean? | *A place where two streets or walls meet | A seat in the classroom | A football kick | Under the bed
test: How many corners does a triangle have? | *3 | 2 | 5 | 4
exam: Which phrase uses corner as a verb meaning trap? | Turn the corner | *To corner a thief | A corner kick | The reading corner
